---
/**
 * UNDERWORLD — the long-form anchor of the page (spec §4.4). Generous
 * layout: multiple prose blocks, pull-quotes, and clearly marked TODO(owner)
 * blocks where the personal essay lands. Themes, in order:
 *   1. Orpheus and Eurydice — the personal anchor
 *   2. Virgil as guide
 *   3. The Furies
 *   4. Soul-marks read after death — sins against kin and nature; greed
 *      stains deepest
 *   5. The Egyptian weighing of the heart against Ma'at's feather
 * Every block carries the owner's essay beneath its classical scaffold.
 */
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import type { SectionSpec } from '../../design/tokens';

interface Props {
  spec: SectionSpec;
}

const { spec } = Astro.props;
---

<Section spec={spec}>
  <h2>Underworld</h2>

  <!-- ── 1. Orpheus and Eurydice — the personal anchor ─────────────── -->
  <article class="block">
    <h3><span data-egg-anchor="underworld-lyre">Orpheus</span>, Looking Back</h3>
    <p class="scaffold">
      I went down singing because singing was the only thing I had that the
      dead would stop for. People forget that part. The lyre was the ticket.
      The ferryman listened. The dog listened. The wheel stopped turning.
      Whatever I carried down there, I carried on the strength of a song. And
      the song was true, which is why it worked, and why it was dangerous. A
      true song makes you believe you've earned what it buys.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      What I carried down was a life I was certain about. Pick the one that
      cost the most; that's the one I mean. I walked it into the dark on
      purpose, the way you carry a body you're not ready to bury, and I
      argued with the house of the dead for it, and I won. Hades gave his
      terms. Walk up. Don't turn. She follows.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      And she did. I need to say that plainly, because the myth skips it: it
      was working. The whole stair up she was there — a second torch behind
      me, heat on the neck, light thrown past my shoulder onto the next step.
      I had everything I'd asked for, in the only form it was ever going to
      come in. Unconfirmed.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      The look back happened at the lip of the light, and it didn't feel like
      doubt. It felt like love. It felt like checking on her. That's how the
      turn gets you — not with fear, with tenderness, and underneath the
      tenderness a demand: prove it. Show me you're real before I've paid the
      whole price. I turned around to collect early on a debt that was still
      being honored, and the collecting is what voided it. Virgil has the
      riverbanks repeating her name down the whole length of the Hebrus. I
      know that sound. It's your own voice coming back.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      <p class="greek" lang="la">
        Eurydicen vox ipsa et frigida lingua,<br />
        a miseram Eurydicen! anima fugiente vocabat:<br />
        Eurydicen toto referebant flumine ripae.
      </p>
      <p class="translation">
        "Eurydice" — the voice itself, the tongue already cold, called with
        fleeing breath: "ah, poor Eurydice!" — and "Eurydice" the banks gave
        back along the whole stream.
      </p>
      <cite>Virgil, Georgics IV.525–527</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="scaffold">
      Here's what the story doesn't tell you, because it ends on the river.
      He didn't lose her at the turn. He lost her the moment he stopped
      trusting the terms. The turn is just when it became visible. Every mark
      I carry reads the same way once you know how to read it: I lost it when
      I needed proof.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote poem-quote">
      <p>
        I am done turning around.<br />
        Orpheus lost her checking for proof —<br />
        but I have learned to walk the long stair up<br />
        reading the dark itself.
      </p>
      <cite><a href="/writings/pharmakon/">Pharmakon, VI</a></cite>
    </blockquote>
  </article>

  <!-- ── 2. Virgil as guide ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <article class="block">
    <h3>The Guide</h3>
    <p class="scaffold">
      Every descent needs a guide who has mapped the dark and kept his
      composure in it. Virgil walks ahead through his own poems first —
      the bees of the Georgics fail, and the beekeeper is sent down after
      the reason.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      I didn't pick Virgil. He was already walking when I got there, and he
      had the only map with the lower floors on it. That's what a guide is.
      Not a teacher. Somebody who went down, kept his composure, and came
      back with his handwriting still legible.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      He gets the bees back by sending the beekeeper to the source of the
      dying, not to a cure. That's the method. When something you built
      fails, the dead have the reason, and you go down and ask. You don't
      patch. You don't rebrand. You descend. And he plants Orpheus in the
      middle of that story on purpose, a warning posted at the trailhead:
      here is a man who went down for the right reason and came up wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      What nobody tells you about the descent is that you go down for one
      thing and the dark has its own agenda. I went down for a life I'd lost.
      What was waiting at the bottom wasn't the life. It was her. The guide's
      job is to get you that far and no farther. Virgil stops at the edge of
      what he has seen, and the honest ones always do. Past that line you
      walk it alone, with her behind you, and you do not turn around. I've
      had guides like that. I try to be one. Go first. Keep your handwriting
      legible. Know where your map runs out.
    </p>
  </article>

  <!-- ── 3. The Furies ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <article class="block">
    <h3>The Kindly Ones</h3>
    <p class="scaffold">
      The Erinyes rise for blood-guilt inside the family: they answer wrongs
      done to kin, and they are older than the Olympian order that tries to
      civilize them. Athens renamed them Eumenides and gave them a home under
      the hill — the debt itself remains payable.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      Here is what pursues. Not the law. The law can be argued with and
      outlasted. What pursues is older than the law and takes no appeals: the
      wrong done inside the house. The Erinyes don't need a corpse. They need
      blood on blood, and every house has it.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      Mine goes back further than most. I spent my teens in a valley my
      fourth great-grandfather settled with his several wives, under the one
      god, in the order the one god prescribed. Five generations deep in that
      soil. And the wrong inside my house is the same wrong inside nearly
      every house on earth now: a boy cut on his first day by people who
      loved him, because their fathers were cut, because the book said so. No
      one should be born into a world of sexual violence, and almost all of
      us were. That's the blood-guilt. Not a crime one person committed. A
      practice the whole house carries, and carries forward, and calls love.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      They hunt by smell. You can change cities and names and the dogs still
      find you, because what they're tracking is on you, not behind you. I
      ran for a while. Everyone does. It is the most exhausting thing a man
      can do and it doesn't work, which is why the oldest plays are about the
      running and not the crime.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      Settlement looks like Athens. Not acquittal. Orestes stands trial, the
      vote ties, the goddess breaks it, and the Furies are not banished.
      They're given a home under the hill, a new name, and an honest share of
      the city, and in exchange they stop hunting. You don't beat them. You
      house them. You pull the practice out at the root in your own line. You
      put your own beliefs on the altar first, and you sit with the
      possibility that you fell for the oldest trick ever played on men. Then
      you pay on the debt, in public, for as long as it takes. The Kindly
      Ones is not irony. It's what they become when a man stops running and
      starts digging.
    </p>
  </article>

  <!-- ── 4. Soul-marks — sins against kin and nature ───────────────── -->
  <article class="block">
    <h3>Marks on the Soul</h3>
    <p class="scaffold">
      The judges read the soul the way a physician reads a body, because
      every act leaves tissue. The scars are legible. And the old sources
      agree on the grading: wrongs against kin and against nature cut
      deepest, and greed stains deepest of all — it dyes the fabric through,
      because greed is the one sin a man commits continuously.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      <p class="greek" lang="la">quisque suos patimur Manis.</p>
      <p class="translation">Each of us suffers his own shade.</p>
      <cite>Virgil, Aeneid VI.743</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote">
      <p>"Your greed stains your soul with the stench of ruin."</p>
      <cite>D. C. A. Hillman, after Aeschylus</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="scaffold">
      The Greek underneath is exact.
      <span class="greek" lang="grc">Δυσωδία</span> — the stench.
      <span class="greek" lang="grc">Ἀπώλεια</span> — the ruin. Pollution
      has an odor in the old cosmology, and the Kindly Ones hunt by smell.
      The stained man carries it into every room he enters, and the dogs of
      the elder law can track it across any distance and any number of
      years.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote poem-quote">
      <p>
        …the little dying<br />
        that drops the man I was<br />
        like a snake drops the winter skin<br />
        in the tall grass, and does not mourn it
      </p>
      <cite><a href="/writings/pharmakon/">Pharmakon, III</a></cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="scaffold">
      I've learned to read marks on the living. Watch anyone long enough — a
      politician, a preacher, a hero, a villain — and the costume slips, and
      underneath it's just a dude, scarred the same ways as everybody. The
      useful question shifts from <em>who is this person</em> to <em>what
      marked them, and what are they still paying on.</em> Then turn the same
      reading on yourself, while the marks can still be worked on. Do the
      examination before the judges do.
    </p>
  </article>

  <!-- ── 5. The weighing of the heart ──────────────────────────────── -->
  <article class="block">
    <h3 data-egg-anchor="underworld-feather">The Feather</h3>
    <p class="scaffold">
      In the Egyptian hall of the Two Truths the heart is set on one pan of
      the balance and the feather of Ma'at on the other. The heart that
      weighs the same as truth passes; the heavy heart is given to the
      devourer. The scale is exact, and the feather is the whole standard.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      The scale doesn't care what you did. That's the part the preachers
      miss. Anubis isn't reading a ledger. He weighs a heart against a
      feather, and the heart either weighs that or it doesn't. The forty-two
      declarations the dead recite in the hall are all negatives. I have not.
      I have not. You don't earn your way across. You arrive light or you
      don't.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      So the question stops being what did I do and becomes what am I still
      carrying. Every mark on this page has a weight, and the weight is never
      the wrong itself. It's the unpaid part. The running. The needing proof.
      The debt without a room. The parts of yourself you never looked at
      because they were cut away before you could. Those are what a heart
      gets heavy with, and the devourer is patient, and the feather is exact.
    </p>
    <p class="scaffold">
      I've stopped trying to get lighter by subtraction. You can't cut enough
      out; that was the whole lie. What makes a heart light is being seen in
      full, not broken, by the one who lives behind it. She does the weighing
      long before Anubis does. It comes in waves. Some days the pan drops.
      Then you build a place big enough to set things down in. A hearth.
      Wide. Where the dead can sit and the living can sit and nobody has to
      fold to fit. That's where the weighing resolves, if it resolves: not in
      the hall, in the house. Which is where the rain comes in.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="pull-quote poem-quote">
      <p>
        I am building the hearth wide. Wide enough<br />
        for two infinities to sit down without folding.
      </p>
      <cite><a href="/writings/pharmakon/">Pharmakon, VI</a></cite>
    </blockquote>
  </article>
</Section>

<style>
  h3 {
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2.4vw, 1.5rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  .block {
    margin-bottom: 4.5rem;
  }

  .block:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .scaffold {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  .pull-quote {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--section-accent, var(--ink-dim));
    padding: 0.4rem 0 0.4rem 1.4rem;
    margin: 1.8rem 0;
  }

  .pull-quote p {
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.2vw, 1.25rem);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .pull-quote .translation {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }

  .pull-quote cite {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-style: normal;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }

  /* Lines borrowed from the Pharmakon poem carry the writings purple. */
  .poem-quote {
    border-left-color: var(--accent-writings);
  }

  .poem-quote cite a {
    color: var(--ink-dim);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-writings) 55%, transparent);
  }

  .poem-quote cite a:hover {
    color: var(--accent-writings);
  }
</style>
